1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,835
You open your feed for something new.

2
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:05,935
Somehow, you keep returning
to the same topics.

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,215
The videos change.

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,535
The interests we explore
can stay surprisingly familiar.

5
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:14,855
We began to worry that our interests
were narrowing without us noticing.

6
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,155
Research gave us a reason
to take that question seriously.

7
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:22,615
A Spotify study linked algorithmic listening
with lower consumption diversity.

8
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:24,935
Other researchers showed, in simulations,

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:28,615
how recommendation feedback loops
can make users' choices more similar

10
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,595
and reduce the value they get.

11
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,395
That raised a question for us:

12
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,915
How can we make unfamiliar interests
easier to discover?

13
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:37,855
This is OtherWise.

14
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:39,915
Other, for another perspective.

15
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:42,195
Wise, for more thoughtful judgment.

16
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:45,235
And an invitation to think otherwise.

17
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:47,470
Start with an interest you choose.

18
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:51,315
OtherWise suggests unfamiliar
but related topics across fields.

19
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,335
You control how far to explore,

20
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,135
from nearby ideas to broader connections.

21
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,335
Follow a topic that catches your attention.

22
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:59,815
Save it when you want to pursue it,

23
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,235
and explore from that new starting point.

24
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:04,455
A galaxy map makes your exploration visible,

25
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,135
so you can revisit discoveries
and choose where to go next.

26
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,195
Existing research gives this direction
an initial basis.

27
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:13,055
In a small user study,

28
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:16,815
Auralist produced more useful,
previously unknown recommendations.

29
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:20,915
Its authors also proposed letting users
adjust the level of novelty.

30
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:25,035
PURS showed how unexpected recommendations
could remain relevant.

31
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:29,910
Our proposed contribution is to bring these ideas
into a general topic explorer:

32
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,175
interests you confirm,

33
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,175
gradual exploration across fields,

34
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,315
and a visual record of your journey.

35
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:38,930
We're developing it as both a product
and a research project.

36
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:39,935
Next, we want to test

37
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:43,735
whether a new discovery today
can grow into a lasting interest.

38
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:46,155
If your research connects with recommendation,

39
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:48,175
learning, or human-computer interaction,

40
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:50,135
we'd love your guidance.

41
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,270
OtherWise. Your world, a little wider.
